{
  "teamId": 113,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Cincinnati Reds opened their season with an immediate gut punch, surrendering a three-run homer on Opening Day that set an ominous tone. Yet the team refused to fold, and a Jake Fraley grand slam in mid-April ignited a spark that carried Cincinnati through a competitive spring. The Reds showed genuine offensive firepower — Elly De La Cruz flashing star power, Rece Hinds announcing himself with a clutch homer, and Christian Encarnacion-Strand emerging as a middle-of-the-order force — while the bullpen, particularly Emilio Pagán, proved to be a persistent liability that threatened to derail everything.\n\nThe summer months told a story of a team fighting itself as much as its opponents. Pagán surrendered crushing blows with alarming regularity, and a brutal stretch in late May through June saw the Cubs, Twins, and Cardinals all inflict damage that could have buried lesser teams. But Cincinnati kept clawing back. Will Benson's walk-off triple in July and Encarnacion-Strand's grand slam against San Diego were the rallying moments of a team that simply would not quit, keeping the Reds in wild card contention deep into the dog days.\n\nThe final stretch of the regular season became a sprint that tested Cincinnati's resolve. Tyler Stephenson emerged as an unlikely late-season hero, clubbing two pivotal home runs in September that kept the Reds' postseason dreams alive. A September 1st collapse against Toronto was immediately answered by Noelvi Marte's clutch two-RBI single, embodying the never-say-die character that defined this roster. The Reds ground their way to 83 wins — just enough to earn a wild card berth on the strength of a rotation anchored by Hunter Greene and an offense that outscored opponents by 35 runs on the year.\n\nOctober arrived and with it a date with destiny against the Los Angeles Dodgers — and the Reds came agonizingly close. Hunter Greene was the right man to start Game 1, but surrendered solo shots to Shohei Ohtani and a Teoscar Hernández three-run blast that proved decisive. Game 2 offered hope — Sal Stewart's clutch single brought two runs home to put Cincinnati back in it — but the Dodgers' depth was simply too much, with Enrique Hernández and Miguel Rojas delivering the killing blows. A bases-loaded force out to end the series crystallized the Reds' season: talented, tenacious, and heartbreakingly close, but not quite over the hump in 2025.",
  "chapters": [
